 "facts": [
  {
   "claim": "The corpse's right hand: X-radiographs show Rembrandt originally painted only a stump instead of a hand; the hand was added later and is now a slightly different colour from the arm",
   "source": "https://www.mauritshuis.nl/en/our-collection/artworks/146-the-anatomy-lesson-of-dr-nicolaes-tulp ; https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4746154 ('the right arm was originally an amputated stump')"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The painting records Dr Nicolaes Tulp's public anatomy lesson in Amsterdam in January 1632 (dated 31 January 1632); public dissections were open to paying visitors",
   "source": "https://www.mauritshuis.nl/en/our-collection/artworks/146-the-anatomy-lesson-of-dr-nicolaes-tulp ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Anatomy_Lesson_of_Dr._Nicolaes_Tulp ; https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4746154"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The body was that of Adriaen Adriaensz, alias Aris Kindt, a thief arrested for stealing a winter coat, sentenced to death and hanged on 31 January 1632, the same day as the lesson",
   "source": "https://www.mauritshuis.nl/en/our-collection/artworks/146-the-anatomy-lesson-of-dr-nicolaes-tulp ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Anatomy_Lesson_of_Dr._Nicolaes_Tulp ; https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4746154"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Commissioned by the surgeons (guild members contributed towards the fee); Rembrandt was 'only twenty-five', shortly after moving from Leiden to Amsterdam",
   "source": "https://www.mauritshuis.nl/en/our-collection/artworks/146-the-anatomy-lesson-of-dr-nicolaes-tulp ; https://www.universiteitleiden.nl/en/dossiers/rembrandt-and-leiden-university/seven-canvases/the-anatomy-lesson-of-dr-nicolaes-tulp"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Tulp chose to be shown starting the dissection with an arm and a hand, departing from customary practice; the museum conjectures he wanted to emulate Vesalius, whose Fabrica (1543) frontispiece shows him with a dissected arm",
   "source": "https://www.mauritshuis.nl/en/our-collection/artworks/146-the-anatomy-lesson-of-dr-nicolaes-tulp"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Kindt, an inveterate thief, 'undoubtedly lost this hand as a result of a previous conviction' (narrated hedged as 'probably')",
   "source": "https://www.mauritshuis.nl/en/our-collection/artworks/146-the-anatomy-lesson-of-dr-nicolaes-tulp"
  },
  {
   "claim": "'One can easily imagine that either Tulp or Rembrandt, feeling that a stump in this prominent place would attract too much attention, decided to add the hand' (narrated as 'the museum thinks')",
   "source": "https://www.mauritshuis.nl/en/our-collection/artworks/146-the-anatomy-lesson-of-dr-nicolaes-tulp"
  },
  {
   "claim": "'Rembrandt's initial inclusion of the stump shows that he had attended the anatomy demonstration'",
   "source": "https://www.mauritshuis.nl/en/our-collection/artworks/146-the-anatomy-lesson-of-dr-nicolaes-tulp"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Bought by the Dutch State in 1828 (32,000 guilders) after King William I blocked its sale; hangs in the Mauritshuis, The Hague (Room 9)",
   "source": "https://www.mauritshuis.nl/en/our-collection/artworks/146-the-anatomy-lesson-of-dr-nicolaes-tulp ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Anatomy_Lesson_of_Dr._Nicolaes_Tulp"
  }
 ],
 "caption": "Look at the dead man's right hand. It's a slightly different colour from his arm, and there's a reason.\n\nOn 31 January 1632, in Amsterdam, Dr Nicolaes Tulp gave his public anatomy lesson on the body of Aris Kindt (Adriaen Adriaensz), a thief hanged that same day for stealing a winter coat. The surgeons hired a 25-year-old newcomer from Leiden to paint it: Rembrandt.\n\nTulp had himself shown opening the arm first, against the usual order, perhaps to echo the great anatomist Vesalius. But X-rays show that Rembrandt first painted Kindt's other arm ending in a stump. The Mauritshuis thinks he had lost the hand as punishment for an earlier crime, and that Tulp or Rembrandt found the stump too distracting, so a hand was painted on top. As the upper paint grew more transparent, the added hand began to show.\n\nThe museum's conclusion: the stump proves Rembrandt was in the room.\n\nP.S. In 1828 the painting was about to be auctioned. King William I stopped the sale, and the Dutch state bought it for 32,000 guilders.\n\n🖤 Would you have noticed the hand?",
 "caption_short": "This dead man had no right hand. X-rays show Rembrandt painted a stump, then gave the hanged thief a new hand. 🖤",
 "youtube_title": "Who gave him a new hand? Rembrandt's Anatomy Lesson of Dr Tulp",
